<div class="article-body">

<p>The honest answer: hydroponics saves you money on groceries, but only if you grow the right crops. Grow lettuce year-round and you win. Grow a few herbs and brag about it, and you have spent $80 to save about $1.50 a month. Nobody tells you the difference until the kit is already on your shelf.</p>

<p>Every grow light ad and every glossy tower video says the same thing. "Grow your own food and slash your grocery bill." It is the oldest hook in the hobby. I believed it. I bought the gear, set up the jars, and waited for the savings to roll in.</p>

<p>Then I actually did the math. And here is the part I want to share before I show you the numbers: the savings are real, they are just not where you expect. The crop that "feels" like it should save money often does not, and the boring one nobody photographs is the one paying your grocery bill. Let me show you the difference.</p>

<h2 id="promise">The Promise Everyone Makes</h2>

<p>I have read a hundred "grow your own food" articles. They all do the same trick. They take the price of a single herb at the store, $3.99, and multiply it by every week of the year, and announce you will save $200 a year by growing basil. It sounds bulletproof. Then you actually grow the basil, and you realize you use about one bunch a month, and $3.99 times 12 is not $200. It is about $48. And you spent more than that on the light.</p>

<p>There are two ways to get this math wrong. The first is overestimating how much you buy. The second is underestimating what the setup costs. When I finally wrote down my actual grocery habits instead of the hopeful ones, everything changed.</p>

<p>Pay attention to this number: the average household throws away around 30 percent of the fresh produce it buys. That is the quiet killer of the whole "grow to save money" plan. If you buy lettuce and forget it in the fridge, growing your own does not fix that habit. It just gives you a faster way to watch it rot.</p>

<h2 id="math">The Real Math on Four Crops</h2>

<p>Let me show you how this actually plays out, with real prices from my grocery store and the real amounts my household eats. I have grown all of these, so the yields are not from a brochure. They are what my jars actually produced.</p>

<p><strong>Basil.</strong> A fresh bunch at the store costs about $2.50 for a small clamshell. My household uses maybe two bunches a month during tomato season. That is $5 a month, or $60 a year. Growing basil constantly, I harvest about a bunch per plant every few weeks. One plant under my light covers my whole need. But here is the catch: basil is cheap to grow and cheap to buy. The big saving is not the basil. It is the fact that fresh basil makes me cook more, and cooking at home is where the real grocery money goes. More on that later.</p>

<p><strong>Lettuce.</strong> This is the star. A head of decent leafy lettuce costs $2.50 to $4 at my store, and a bag of pre-washed salad mix costs more. My household eats about two lettuce-heavy salads a week, which means I was spending $8 to $12 a week on lettuce and salad greens. That is $400 to $600 a year. Pay attention to this number: a single Kratky jar grows you a full lettuce head in about 6 weeks, and I run six jars at a time in rotation. That is a head of lettuce every week, roughly, for a seed cost of a few cents. The math is not close. Lettuce is the crop that pays for the whole hobby.</p>

<p><strong>Microgreens.</strong> These are the trendy one. A box of microgreens at the store costs $4 to $6 and lasts a few meals. Grow them yourself and a $6 tray of seed yields several times that. I grew pea shoots and sunflower shoots for a while. The AMOUNT you save is real, around 5x on price. The amount it MATTERS to your grocery bill is small, because you are not eating six boxes of microgreens a week. It is a rounding error on a food budget. Fun, impressive, not a money saver.</p>

<p><strong>Cherry tomatoes.</strong> A pint costs $3 to $4, and in season I used to buy one a week. That is $150 to $200 a year, and they never last long enough. A single cherry tomato plant under a grow light gives you more tomatoes than you can finish, for months. This is the second-best money crop after lettuce, because the store price is high and the yield is enormous. And the taste difference is not even close. Store cherry tomatoes are picked green and gas-ripened. Mine hang on the vine until they are actually red.</p>

<p>So the honest ranking, by real savings: lettuce and salad greens first, then cherry tomatoes, then herbs as a small bonus, then microgreens as a hobby that tastes great but does not move the bill.</p>

<h2 id="winners">The Crops That Actually Pay You Back</h2>

<p>Here is the pattern behind the winners. The crops that save you money share three traits. They are expensive at the store. Your household eats a lot of them. And they travel poorly, meaning the store version is either pricey or tasteless.</p>

<p>Lettuce hits all three. It is heavy and water-filled, so the store charges a premium for shipping all that water around in a plastic bag. It goes bad fast, so it gets marked up and then marked down. And most households eat a surprising amount of it. I went into the full growing method in my <a href="/posts/grow-lettuce-indoors-year-round">how to grow lettuce indoors year-round</a> guide, but the money point is simple: this is the crop that makes the hobby pay for itself.</p>

<p>Cherry tomatoes are second. High store price, huge household consumption, and the store version is objectively worse. One plant running for months under a decent light produces a steady pint or two a week. That is $150 a year of groceries from a $5 seed packet and an ongoing light cost. I wrote my exact seed-to-harvest setup in my <a href="/posts/how-to-grow-cherry-tomatoes-indoors">how to grow cherry tomatoes indoors</a> guide.</p>

<p>Here is the key insight: the money is not in growing everything. It is in growing the two or three things your household already buys most, and letting the store keep the rest until you have spare capacity. You do not need to grow kale, spinach, and three herbs to save money. You need one lettuce rotation and maybe a tomato plant.</p>

<h2 id="losers">The Crops That Quietly Lose You Money</h2>

<p>Now the honest part, because the "save money" crowd never talks about it. Several popular hydroponic crops are money losers, and I grew them all before I noticed the pattern.</p>

<p><strong>Microgreens.</strong> I covered the math above. Cheap to buy, and you do not eat enough to matter. You will spend more on the seed, the peat, and the extra trays than you save. Grow them because they are delicious and make your food look like a restaurant, not to save money.</p>

<p><strong>Herbs in general.</strong> A single basil or mint plant is great. But your household uses about one fresh herb bundle a week at most, and the store version is $2.50. The saving is real but tiny, a few dollars a month. The same light that grows one basil plant could be growing a lettuce jar that saves you five times as much. Herbs are a luxury you grow because you like them, not a savings engine.</p>

<p><strong>Kale and other hearty greens.</strong> Store kale is cheap, around $2 a bunch, and a single plant yields a lot. But you eat less kale than lettuce, and it takes longer to mature, so your jars sit occupied for weeks producing a crop you could have bought for $2. The opportunity cost is the killer: every jar growing slow cheap greens is a jar not growing fast expensive lettuce.</p>

<p><strong>Anything you already throw away.</strong> This is the real loser, and it applies to every crop. If you bought store lettuce and threw half of it out, growing your own does not save that money. It just changes which half rots. Track your household for two weeks first. Grow only what you actually finish. I cannot stress this enough, because it is the difference between saving $400 a year and saving $40.</p>

<p>Here is why that matters. The money in growing food is not "free food." It is replacing the two or three priciest things you already eat with versions that cost pennies. If you grow the wrong crop, or grow a crop you do not eat, you have built a hobby, not a savings plan. Both are fine. Just do not confuse them.</p>

<h2 id="numbers">My Own Grocery Bill, Tracked</h2>

<p>Let me not hide behind theory. Here is my real grocery math from the last year, since I started my current rotation. I keep my receipts, so these are what I actually spent, not what I hoped.</p>

<p>Before I set up my jars, I was spending about $52 a month on produce from the grocery store. The bulk of that was the same few things: lettuce and salad mix at roughly $10 a week, cherry tomatoes at $3 once a week, and a rotating set of herbs and greens.</p>

<p>Here is the breakdown of what that turned into after I had a working setup.</p>
<p>1. Lettuce and salad greens dropped from about $40 a month to about $6, and that $6 is only the red leaf and arugula seeds I could not be bothered to grow.</p>
<p>2. Cherry tomatoes dropped from $13 a month to about zero, because one plant kept my household in tomatoes from month three onward.</p>
<p>3. Herbs dropped from $8 a month to about zero. I now have basil and mint going constantly under a second small light.</p>
<p>My produce spend went from $52 a month to roughly $14 a month. That is a saving of about $38 a month, or roughly $450 a year, from a setup whose light cost about $85 once and a nutrient bottle I replace for $15 every few months.</p>

<p>Here is the honest truth: that $450 number is the best case, and it depends on one thing I almost did not mention. I grow lettuce in a real rotation, six jars at a time, so there is always a head ready. If I had grown microgreens and a single basil plant and called it a day, my saving would have been maybe $30 a month or nothing. The setup is identical. The crop choice is everything. That is the whole secret, and it is why most people's towers save them nothing: they grow the pretty stuff, not the expensive stuff.</p>

<h2 id="decision">How to Decide What to Grow</h2>

<p>So here is how I would decide, if you actually want the grocery savings instead of just the hobby.</p>

<p>First, for two weeks, write down what produce you buy and what you throw away. Do not guess. Guessing is how every "save money" article sells you a fantasy. You will almost certainly be surprised at which line is biggest. In my house it was lettuce, and I had no idea until I wrote it down.</p>

<p>Second, pick the single biggest line you consistently finish, and grow that first. For most households it is lettuce or salad greens. That one crop, done well and rotated, covers your light cost within a couple of months and then starts saving you real money. I walked through the exact setup I run in my <a href="/posts/cheapest-way-to-start-hydroponics">cheapest way to start hydroponics</a> guide, and the nutrition basics so you never starve your plants are in my <a href="/posts/hydroponic-nutrient-guide">hydroponic nutrient guide</a>.</p>

<p>Third, add a second expensive-to-buy crop you actually eat. Cherry tomatoes are the obvious one. If you do not eat them, pick whatever your receipt shows you spending the most on, as long as it grows indoors and you finish it.</p>

<p>Fourth, treat everything else as a bonus, not a goal. Grow herbs because they make dinner better. Grow microgreens because they are beautiful. But never pretend those small crops are why the hobby is worth it. Let them be the dessert and let lettuce be the mortgage.</p>

<p>Here is the part that matters. Growing food does not automatically save you money. Growing the two or three things you already spend the most on, and actually eating them, absolutely does. That is the difference between a shelf of pretty plants and a real drop in your grocery bill. In my house it is about $450 a year, and it started with one boring jar of lettuce that paid me back before the basil even mattered. Start there. Let your receipt tell you what to grow next.</p>

